## Solution

**Numerator:** $\displaystyle\int_0^1 x^{-2/3}(1-x)^{-1/3}\,dx$ is a Beta integral. With $p-1=-2/3\Rightarrow p=\tfrac13$ and $q-1=-\tfrac13\Rightarrow q=\tfrac23$,

$$\int_0^1 x^{-2/3}(1-x)^{-1/3}\,dx = B\!\left(\tfrac13,\tfrac23\right)=\frac{\Gamma(\tfrac13)\Gamma(\tfrac23)}{\Gamma(1)}=\Gamma\!\left(\tfrac13\right)\Gamma\!\left(\tfrac23\right).$$

By the reflection formula $\Gamma(z)\Gamma(1-z)=\dfrac{\pi}{\sin(\pi z)}$ with $z=\tfrac13$,

$$\Gamma\!\left(\tfrac13\right)\Gamma\!\left(\tfrac23\right)=\frac{\pi}{\sin(\pi/3)}=\frac{\pi}{\sqrt{3}/2}=\frac{2\pi}{\sqrt{3}}.$$

**Denominator:** $\displaystyle\int_0^\infty x^{1/2}e^{-x^3}\,dx$. Substitute $u=x^3$, so $x=u^{1/3}$, $dx=\tfrac13 u^{-2/3}\,du$, and $x^{1/2}=u^{1/6}$:

$$\int_0^\infty x^{1/2}e^{-x^3}\,dx=\int_0^\infty u^{1/6}e^{-u}\cdot\tfrac13 u^{-2/3}\,du=\frac13\int_0^\infty u^{-1/2}e^{-u}\,du=\frac13\,\Gamma\!\left(\tfrac12\right)=\frac{\sqrt{\pi}}{3}.$$

**Ratio:**

$$\frac{\,2\pi/\sqrt{3}\,}{\,\sqrt{\pi}/3\,}=\frac{2\pi}{\sqrt{3}}\cdot\frac{3}{\sqrt{\pi}}=\frac{6\pi}{\sqrt{3}\sqrt{\pi}}=\frac{6\sqrt{\pi}}{\sqrt{3}}=2\sqrt{3}\sqrt{\pi}=2\sqrt{3\pi}.$$

$$\boxed{\dfrac{\int_0^1 x^{-2/3}(1-x)^{-1/3}\,dx}{\int_0^\infty x^{1/2}e^{-x^3}\,dx}=2\sqrt{3\pi}}$$

